WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:03.433
Data analysis is full of small questions:

2
00:00:03.433 --> 00:00:07.195
is this bigger, is this smaller, are these the same?

3
00:00:07.195 --> 00:00:12.461
In this lesson, you will learn the six comparison operators in R, and how

4
00:00:12.461 --> 00:00:15.471
R answers each question with TRUE or FALSE.

5
00:00:15.471 --> 00:00:18.480
It is easier than it sounds. Let's begin.

6
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:25.857
Here I wrote a short list of notes, so we can see all six operators together.

7
00:00:25.857 --> 00:00:30.632
Less than, greater than, less than or equal to, greater than or equal to,

8
00:00:30.632 --> 00:00:33.360
is equal to, and is not equal to.

9
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:43.435
Let's try them one by one, with the same two numbers:

10
00:00:43.435 --> 00:00:44.720
twenty five and fifty.

11
00:00:45.900 --> 00:00:49.529
Twenty five, less than, fifty. Run it. R says TRUE.

12
00:00:49.529 --> 00:00:50.980
Twenty five is smaller.

13
00:00:51.900 --> 00:00:54.180
Twenty five, greater than, fifty. FALSE.

14
00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:56.460
Of course, it is not bigger.

15
00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.041
Now less than or equal to.

16
00:01:02.041 --> 00:01:05.609
We write the less than sign, then the equals sign.

17
00:01:05.609 --> 00:01:08.820
Twenty five, less than or equal to, fifty: TRUE.

18
00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:11.360
Greater than or equal to: FALSE.

19
00:01:14.900 --> 00:01:17.560
Is equal to, with two equals signs:

20
00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:19.840
FALSE, because the numbers are different.

21
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:26.815
And is not equal to, with an exclamation mark and an equals sign.

22
00:01:26.815 --> 00:01:29.840
Twenty five is not equal to fifty: TRUE.

23
00:01:34.900 --> 00:01:38.386
Take a moment to read the answers in the console.

24
00:01:38.386 --> 00:01:40.477
Every question gives TRUE or FALSE.

25
00:01:40.477 --> 00:01:44.660
We will use these questions a lot later, when we filter data.

26
00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:52.650
Next, I add a new note: calling a function.

27
00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:55.094
A function is a ready-made tool in R.

28
00:01:55.094 --> 00:01:58.760
You give it some values, and it does the work for you.

29
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.681
Our first function is sort.

30
00:02:11.681 --> 00:02:15.601
Inside the brackets, I put a vector of numbers with c.

31
00:02:15.601 --> 00:02:19.520
Run it, and R lists the numbers from smallest to largest.

32
00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:42.399
Then sum, which adds them all up.

33
00:02:42.399 --> 00:02:45.613
The answer is five thousand five hundred ninety four.

34
00:02:45.613 --> 00:02:49.540
We will meet more functions in the next lesson. Well done!

